    <!-- Guided Tutorial (ui/tutorial) -->
    <string name="tut_welcome_title">Welcome to Repertoire Manager!</string>
    <string name="tut_welcome_text">Repertoire Manager is your personal companion for your music repertoire: manage your pieces, track your learning progress bar by bar, practice with a timer and metronome, and keep all your chord sheets in one place. This tour walks you through the most important features step by step – and you\'ll try the key ones yourself.</string>
    <string name="tut_welcome_language">Choose language</string>
    <string name="tut_welcome_language_hint">You can change the language at any time in Settings.</string>
    <string name="tut_welcome_start">Start the tour</string>
    <string name="tut_welcome_skip">Skip tutorial</string>
    <string name="tut_skip_all">End tutorial</string>
    <string name="tut_skip_step">Skip this step</string>
    <string name="tut_hint_action">👆 Tap the highlighted element to continue.</string>
    <string name="tut_choice_yes">Yes, show me chords</string>
    <string name="tut_choice_no">No, end the tutorial</string>
    <string name="tut_repertoire_intro_title">Your repertoire</string>
    <string name="tut_repertoire_intro_text">This is the overview of all your pieces. To get you started, two Beethoven example pieces are already set up. Each card shows the title, composer, status (New, Learning, Polishing or Repertoire), key and the difficulty as dots. Pieces are grouped by status: “In progress”, “Repertoire” and “New”.</string>
    <string name="tut_repertoire_progress_title">The progress bar</string>
    <string name="tut_repertoire_progress_text">The colored bar shows at a glance how far along you are with a piece. It is made up of the piece\'s sections, and each color is a learning level: gray = not learned yet, red = learning, turquoise = polishing, green = mastered. The percentage on the right shows how much of the piece is already “Mastered” (green).</string>
    <string name="tut_repertoire_filters_title">Filtering</string>
    <string name="tut_repertoire_filters_text">Use these chips to filter your repertoire by status, tags, epoch, or instrument. Tap a chip to filter, and tap it again to remove the filter. In Settings, you can configure which instruments are shown in the app! You assign your own tags (e.g. #recital) when editing a piece.</string>
    <string name="tut_repertoire_topbar_title">Tools at the top</string>
    <string name="tut_repertoire_topbar_text">The top bar has four helpers: Shuffle picks a random piece to practice, the music-note icon opens the metronome, the gear opens Settings (language, epochs, backups and this tutorial) and the magnifier searches by title or composer. Pieces you haven\'t practiced for a while are also suggested under “Time to refresh!”.</string>
    <string name="tut_repertoire_nav_title">The navigation bar</string>
    <string name="tut_repertoire_nav_text">Down here you switch between the areas: Repertoire (where you are now), Practice (timer, focus mode, streak and your practice log), Lists (e.g. programs for recitals), Statistics (practice time, epochs, composers) and Chords (chord sheets). After this tour you can explore everything at your own pace.</string>
    <string name="tut_add_fab_title">Add a new piece</string>
    <string name="tut_add_fab_text">You add new pieces with the green plus button. Try it right away: tap the plus.</string>
    <string name="tut_add_openopus_title">Quick start with OpenOpus</string>
    <string name="tut_add_openopus_text">Type the name of a composer or work here, e.g. “Beethoven”. The app searches the OpenOpus database (internet connection required) and fills in title and composer for you as soon as you tap a result.</string>
    <string name="tut_add_details_title">The piece details</string>
    <string name="tut_add_details_text">Enter the title and composer here (required fields), plus difficulty, epoch, tags, and instrument. You can assign an instrument to each piece.</string>
    <string name="tut_add_progress_title">Status and target tempo</string>
    <string name="tut_add_progress_text">Set the piece\'s status – New, Learning or Repertoire – and optionally a target tempo in BPM. You\'ll see the target tempo later on the piece page; your actual tempo goes into the tempo log.</string>
    <string name="tut_add_sheets_title">Sheet music and bar count</string>
    <string name="tut_add_sheets_text">Attach your sheet music as a PDF or image here – it will then be available right inside the app. If you enter the total number of bars, the progress bar can show your learning status correctly. Don\'t know the bar count? No problem: the automatic bar detection can work it out for you later.</string>
    <string name="tut_add_save_title">Saving</string>
    <string name="tut_add_save_text">“Save” at the top right creates the piece. We won\'t save anything during this tutorial – just tap “Next” and we\'ll head back to the overview.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_open_card_title">Open a piece</string>
    <string name="tut_a_open_card_text">Let\'s take a closer look at a piece. Tap the card of Beethoven\'s “Pathétique”.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_detail_header_title">The piece page</string>
    <string name="tut_a_detail_header_text">At the top you see the title, composer, status and difficulty. “Start” starts a practice timer – when you stop it, the session is saved automatically. “Switch to Focus Mode” opens a distraction-free practice screen with a metronome. The pencil at the top right lets you edit all details of the piece.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_detail_progress_title">Progress</string>
    <string name="tut_a_detail_progress_text">This is the same progress bar as in the overview – just bigger. Below it you\'ll find your target tempo, if you\'ve set one.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_detail_sections_title">Sections</string>
    <string name="tut_a_detail_sections_text">A piece consists of sections, e.g. “Takt 1–10” (bars 1–10). Each section has a learning level (gray, red, turquoise, green) – the progress bar is calculated from them. Tap a section to change its name, bars, level and notes. “+ Section” adds a new one, “Auto-Split” divides the piece into equally long sections automatically. The most convenient way to create sections is directly in the sheet music – I\'ll show you in a moment.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_detail_tempo_title">Tempo log</string>
    <string name="tut_a_detail_tempo_text">With “+ Entry” you record how fast you last played the piece (in BPM) – optionally with a note. That way you can see your progress in black and white.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_open_sheets_title">On to the sheet music</string>
    <string name="tut_a_open_sheets_text">In the “Sheet music &amp; PDF” area you attach PDFs or images to your piece. Now tap “View &amp; Edit Sheet Music” to open the score.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_sheet_top_title">The sheet music screen</string>
    <string name="tut_a_sheet_top_text">At the top you see the piece title, bar count and current page. At the top right are buttons for Phone Mode (landscape single-row view of all bars), full screen, practice sessions, and auto-scrolling. Use two fingers to zoom in.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_sheet_tools_title">The tools</string>
    <string name="tut_a_sheet_tools_text">Your tools are at the bottom: “Scroll” for scrolling and selecting bars, “Pen” and “Highlighter” for notes (with color choices), “Eraser”, “Bar” for bar detection and corrections, and “Sections” to show or hide the section display.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_sheet_sections_title">Sections in the score</string>
    <string name="tut_a_sheet_sections_text">This bar lists all sections of the Pathétique with their bars and learning level. Once bars have been detected (we\'ll get to that with the second piece), the sections are also marked in color directly on the score – so you can see right away which passages still need work.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_hide_sections_title">Hide the sections</string>
    <string name="tut_a_hide_sections_text">Sometimes you want to see the score without any markings. Tap “Sections” to hide the display.</string>
    <string name="tut_a_sections_done_title">Done!</string>
    <string name="tut_a_sections_done_text">The bar is gone. Tap again to show the sections once more. The sections themselves are kept – only the display changes. On to the second piece.</string>
    <string name="tut_b_open_card_title">The second piece</string>
    <string name="tut_b_open_card_text">Now I\'ll show you the automatic bar detection. For that we\'ll open the second example piece: tap the “Moonlight” sonata.</string>
    <string name="tut_b_open_sheets_title">Open the score</string>
    <string name="tut_b_open_sheets_text">Open the score here too via “View &amp; Edit Sheet Music”.</string>
    <string name="tut_b_detect_intro_title">Automatic bar detection</string>
    <string name="tut_b_detect_intro_text">To create sections and track your progress bar by bar, the app has to know where each bar starts and ends. The bar detection does this automatically: it looks for the staff systems and barlines in your score scan and numbers every bar. You\'ll find it in the “Bar” tool.</string>
    <string name="tut_b_detect_limits_title">Good to know</string>
    <string name="tut_b_detect_limits_text">The bar detection is currently designed for piano systems: two connected staves are recognized as one system. The cleaner and straighter the scan, the better it works. We\'re working on extending the feature to other instruments as well.</string>
    <string name="tut_b_select_edit_title">Choose the bar tool</string>
    <string name="tut_b_select_edit_text">Now tap “Bar” to open the editing mode.</string>
    <string name="tut_b_run_detect_title">Start the detection</string>
    <string name="tut_b_run_detect_text">Now for the exciting part: tap “Bar detection”. It only takes a moment.</string>
    <string name="tut_b_result_title">The result</string>
    <string name="tut_b_result_text">The app has drawn in the staff systems and barlines: green and red lines frame the systems, blue lines separate the bars. Once bars are detected, you can also use Phone Mode at the top to scroll through all bars in a single row in landscape view!</string>
    <string name="tut_b_edit_tools_title">Correcting mistakes</string>
    <string name="tut_b_edit_tools_text">If something was detected wrongly, you can fix it yourself: “System +” adds a missing staff system (tap its middle), “Barline +” adds a missing barline, and “Delete” removes wrongly detected systems or barlines. You can also tap a line and move it with a long press and drag.</string>
    <string name="tut_b_create_sections_title">Creating sections</string>
    <string name="tut_b_create_sections_text">Switch back to “Scroll”. Now you can tap bars (or long-press and drag across several bars) to select them. A bar with “Create section” appears at the top: give it a name and a learning level – and the section shows up in color in the score, in the progress bar and on the piece page.</string>
    <string name="tut_c_ask_title">Chords – do you need them?</string>
    <string name="tut_c_ask_text">Besides sheet music, Repertoire Manager also manages chord sheets for guitar, piano and more. Do you want to use this feature? If not, we\'ll head straight to Statistics!</string>
    <string name="tut_c_add_title">Adding songs</string>
    <string name="tut_c_add_text">The plus button adds chord sheets. There are several ways: import a whole folder, send a song directly via the share menu from the Ultimate Guitar app to Repertoire Manager, paste UG text, or write a sheet manually in ChordPro format (e.g. [C]text). At the top you search for songs, mark favorites and sort them into playlists. In a song sheet the app can scroll along automatically. Next, let\'s look at the Ultimate Guitar import.</string>
    <string name="tut_c_ug_meta_title">Ultimate Guitar: title &amp; artist</string>
    <string name="tut_c_ug_meta_text">Here\'s how to bring a song over from Ultimate Guitar: open the song there in the app or browser. The easiest way is to tap \'Share\' and select Repertoire Manager – the song will be imported instantly! Alternatively, you can copy the text and paste it here. Here you first enter the title and artist.</string>
    <string name="tut_c_ug_text_title">Paste the text</string>
    <string name="tut_c_ug_text_text">Paste the copied text into this large field. The app recognizes which lines are chords and converts everything to ChordPro format automatically – the chords then sit at the right spot in the lyrics.</string>
    <string name="tut_c_ug_save_title">Save</string>
    <string name="tut_c_ug_save_text">“Save” puts the song into your collection, and you can quickly find it again with the “UG Import” filter. We won\'t save anything now – just tap “Next”.</string>
    <string name="tut_c_db_title">Importing whole databases</string>
    <string name="tut_c_db_text">The folder icon imports whole collections at once: pick a folder with song files in .pro, .crd or .chopro format – the app also searches all subfolders and builds an index (you can rebuild it in Settings). For copyright reasons Repertoire Manager does not ship any song collections. One well-known example is the OLGA archive (OnLine Guitar Archive); you\'ll find similar collections online. Please respect the terms of use of each archive. Download a collection, unpack it and select the folder here.</string>
    <string name="tut_stats_intro_title">Statistics</string>
    <string name="tut_stats_intro_text">Here you can see your practice progress, habits, and distribution of pieces. Use the edit icon at the top right to customize which tiles you want to see and hide unused ones.</string>
    <string name="tut_finish_title">That\'s it! 🎉</string>
    <string name="tut_finish_text">You now know the most important features of Repertoire Manager. Have fun practicing and making music! You can replay this tour at any time in Settings under “Start Tutorial”.</string>

